  "slug": "nasutoceratops",
  "summary": "Nasutoceratops was a horned dinosaur that lived in Utah about 76–75.5 million years ago. Its fossils were found in the Kaiparowits Formation from 2006 onward, including a nearly complete subadult skull, partial skeleton and rare skin impressions. The genus and species, Nasutoceratops titusi, were named in 2013; the species honors paleontologist Alan Titus.\n\nThe dinosaur was about 4.5 m long and 1.5 tonnes. Its short, deep snout and unusually large nostril region were distinctive. Its brow horns reached 457 mm, the longest known among centrosaurines, and curved forward and outward before turning upward. The nasal horn was low and blade-shaped; the nearly circular neck frill had low, crescent-shaped edge bones.\n\nNasutoceratops was an early-diverging centrosaurine and may have belonged to its own subgroup. Its horns may have served in signalling or combat, while the function of its deep snout is uncertain. It lived in a wetland environment on Laramidia, alongside other ceratopsians. Its fossils have also informed debate over whether Laramidia had distinct dinosaur provinces.",
